    long total = 0;
    for (int q = 0; q < 100000; q++) total += count_in_range(big, q, q + 1);
    CHECK_EQ("100,000 range queries", total, 800000L);
}

steps.text:
On sorted data, two indexes moving toward each other can often replace a nested loop. Each comparison lets you **rule out one element for good**, so the whole scan is O(n) instead of O(n²).

Here's the idea on a different problem: is a string a palindrome (the same forwards and backwards), ignoring non-letters?

```cpp
#include <cctype>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

bool is_palindrome(const std::string& s) {
    int i = 0, j = (int)s.size() - 1;
    while (i < j) {
        if (!std::isalpha((unsigned char)s[i])) { i++; continue; }
        if (!std::isalpha((unsigned char)s[j])) { j--; continue; }
        if (std::tolower((unsigned char)s[i]) != std::tolower((unsigned char)s[j])) return false;
        i++;
        j--;
    }
    return true;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << is_palindrome("Never odd or even") << is_palindrome("A man, a plan, a canal: Panama")
              << is_palindrome("two pointers") << "\n";
}
```

```text
110
```

### Two pointers for a target sum

For "do two numbers in a sorted list add up to `target`?":

- start with `i` at the smallest value and `j` at the largest,
- if `v[i] + v[j]` is **too small**, the only way to grow the sum is to move `i` right (`j` is already the biggest),
- if it's **too big**, move `j` left,
- if it's equal, you've found a pair.

Every step discards one value that can't be part of any answer, so the loop runs at most n times. Stop when `i` meets `j`, because the two positions must be different.

Watch the types: `target` is a `long`, and two big `int`s can overflow when added. Add them as `long`: `(long)v[i] + v[j]`.

**Your turn:** write `bool pair_sum(const std::vector<int>& sorted, long target)` that says whether two **different positions** hold values summing to `target`.

steps.seed:
#include <vector>

bool pair_sum(const std::vector<int>& sorted, long target) {

}

steps.solution:
#include <vector>

bool pair_sum(const std::vector<int>& sorted, long target) {
    if (sorted.size() < 2) return false;
    std::size_t i = 0, j = sorted.size() - 1;
    while (i < j) {
        long s = static_cast<long>(sorted[i]) + sorted[j];
        if (s == target) return true;
        if (s < target) i++;
        else j--;
    }
    return false;
}

steps.harness:
int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {1, 4, 6, 9, 13};
    CHECK_EQ("4 + 9", pair_sum(v, 13), true);
    CHECK_EQ("no pair makes 3", pair_sum(v, 3), false);
    CHECK_EQ("can't use 6 twice", pair_sum(v, 12), false);
    CHECK_EQ("duplicates at two positions", pair_sum({5, 5}, 10), true);
    CHECK_EQ("single element", pair_sum({7}, 14), false);
    std::vector<int> big(300000);
    for (int i = 0; i < 300000; i++) big[i] = 3 * i;
    CHECK_EQ("large: no pair sums to 1", pair_sum(big, 1), false);
    CHECK_EQ("large: ends", pair_sum(big, 3L * 299999 + 3), true);
}

steps.text:
If you'll ask "what's the sum of elements l..r?" many times, adding them up each time costs O(n) per question. Instead, precompute **prefix sums** once: `pre[i]` is the sum of the first `i` elements. Then any range sum is one subtraction.

```
v   =     3   1   4   1   5
pre = 0   3   4   8   9  14

sum(1..3) = pre[4] - pre[1] = 9 - 3 = 6     (1 + 4 + 1)
```

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> rain = {3, 0, 12, 5, 0, 7, 2};       // mm per day
    std::vector<long long> pre(rain.size() + 1, 0);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rain.size(); i++) pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + rain[i];

    auto total = [&pre](int l, int r) { return pre[r + 1] - pre[l]; };   // inclusive
    std::cout << "days 0-6: " << total(0, 6) << "\n";
    std::cout << "days 2-3: " << total(2, 3) << "\n";
    std::cout << "day 4: " << total(4, 4) << "\n";
}
```

```text
days 0-6: 29
days 2-3: 17
day 4: 0
```

### How it works

- `pre` has **one more** entry than the data, with `pre[0] = 0`. That extra zero means a range starting at index 0 needs no special case.
- `pre[r + 1]` is the sum of everything up to and including `r`; subtracting `pre[l]` removes everything before `l`.
- Use `long long` for the sums: 200,000 values can easily add up past the `int` limit.

O(n) setup, then O(1) per query. The same trick works for counts ("how many errors between 9:00 and 9:15?"), 2D grids (summed-area tables, used in image processing) and time series.

**Your turn:** write class `RangeSum` with a constructor taking the values and `long long sum(int l, int r) const` for the inclusive range. The test runs 200,000 queries on 200,000 values.

steps.seed:
#include <vector>

class RangeSum {
public:
    explicit RangeSum(const std::vector<int>& v);
    long long sum(int l, int r) const;

private:

};

steps.solution:
#include <vector>

class RangeSum {
public:
    explicit RangeSum(const std::vector<int>& v);
    long long sum(int l, int r) const;

private:
    std::vector<long long> pre_;
};

RangeSum::RangeSum(const std::vector<int>& v) : pre_(v.size() + 1, 0) {
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) pre_[i + 1] = pre_[i] + v[i];
}

long long RangeSum::sum(int l, int r) const {
    return pre_[r + 1] - pre_[l];
}

steps.harness:
int main() {
    RangeSum rs({3, -1, 4, 1, 5, 9});
    CHECK_EQ("whole", rs.sum(0, 5), 21LL);
    CHECK_EQ("middle", rs.sum(1, 3), 4LL);
    CHECK_EQ("single", rs.sum(4, 4), 5LL);
    std::vector<int> big(200000);
    for (int i = 0; i < 200000; i++) big[i] = i % 100;
    RangeSum b(big);
    long long total = 0;
    for (int q = 0; q < 200000; q++) total += b.sum(q % 1000, 199999 - q % 1000);
    CHECK("200,000 queries finish", total > 0);
    CHECK_EQ("big sum", b.sum(0, 199999), 9900000LL);
}

steps.text:
A **sliding window** keeps a range `[left, right]` of the data that only ever moves forward. Each element enters the window once and leaves once, so the whole scan is O(n), even though it effectively examines a huge number of ranges.

Here's a window that grows and shrinks: the shortest stretch of days whose rainfall reaches a target.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int shortest_reaching(const std::vector<int>& v, int target) {
    int best = 0, sum = 0, left = 0;
    for (int right = 0; right < (int)v.size(); right++) {
        sum += v[right];                        // grow: v[right] enters
        while (sum >= target) {                 // shrink while still valid
            int len = right - left + 1;
            if (best == 0 || len < best) best = len;
            sum -= v[left];                     // v[left] leaves
            left++;
        }
    }
    return best;                                // 0 means never reached
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> rain = {2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2, 8};
    std::cout << shortest_reaching(rain, 8) << "\n";    // just the 8 at the end
    std::cout << shortest_reaching(rain, 9) << "\n";    // 5 1 3, or 2 8
    std::cout << shortest_reaching(rain, 100) << "\n";
}
```

```text
1
2
0
```

### The pattern

1. Move `right` forward one step, adding the new element to the window's state.
2. While the window breaks (or satisfies) the condition, move `left` forward, removing elements from the state.
3. Record the answer at the right moment.

`left` never moves backwards, and `right` goes through the data once, so the total work is O(n).

### Your task: longest substring with no repeats

The window's state is "the last position where each character was seen", kept in an array of 256 ints set to -1. When `s[right]` was last seen **inside** the window (at a position `>= left`), jump `left` to one past that position. Then record `s[right]`'s new position and update the best length `right - left + 1`. Index the array with `(unsigned char)s[right]` so characters above 127 don't give a negative index.

**Your turn:** write `int longest_unique(const std::string& s)`: the length of the longest substring with **no repeated characters**. For `"abcabcbb"` it's 3 (`"abc"`).

Idea: move `right` forward one character at a time. If that character already appears inside the window, jump `left` past its previous position. Remember each character's last position in an array of 256 ints.

steps.seed:
#include <string>

int longest_unique(const std::string& s) {

}

steps.solution:
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <string>

int longest_unique(const std::string& s) {
    std::array<int, 256> last;
    last.fill(-1);
    int best = 0, left = 0;
    for (int right = 0; right < static_cast<int>(s.size()); right++) {
        unsigned char c = static_cast<unsigned char>(s[right]);
        if (last[c] >= left) left = last[c] + 1;
        last[c] = right;
        best = std::max(best, right - left + 1);
    }
    return best;
}

steps.harness:
int main() {
    CHECK_EQ("abcabcbb", longest_unique("abcabcbb"), 3);
    CHECK_EQ("bbbbb", longest_unique("bbbbb"), 1);
    CHECK_EQ("pwwkew", longest_unique("pwwkew"), 3);
    CHECK_EQ("empty", longest_unique(""), 0);
    CHECK_EQ("abba", longest_unique("abba"), 2);
    CHECK_EQ("all different", longest_unique("dust2"), 5);
    std::string big;
    for (int i = 0; i < 400000; i++) big += static_cast<char>('a' + i % 26);
    CHECK_EQ("400,000 characters", longest_unique(big), 26);
}

steps.text:
You'll almost always sort with `std::sort` in real code. But knowing **how** sorting works teaches the core ideas of algorithm design (growing a solved part, divide and conquer, partitioning), and interviewers love asking about it. This module builds the classic algorithms yourself.

**Insertion sort** grows a sorted prefix, like sorting cards in your hand: take the next card and slide it left past every larger card until its spot opens up.

```
for i in 1..n-1:
    key = v[i]; j = i - 1
    while j >= 0 and v[j] > key: v[j + 1] = v[j]; j--
    v[j + 1] = key
```

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

void show(const std::vector<std::string>& v) {
    for (const auto& s : v) std::cout << s << " ";
    std::cout << "\n";
}

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> hand = {"7", "3", "9", "2"};
    show(hand);
    for (std::size_t i = 1; i < hand.size(); i++) {
        std::string key = hand[i];
        int j = (int)i - 1;
        while (j >= 0 && std::stoi(hand[j]) > std::stoi(key)) {
            hand[j + 1] = hand[j];          // shift the bigger card right
            j--;
        }
        hand[j + 1] = key;                  // drop the key into the gap
        show(hand);
    }
}
```

```text
7 3 9 2 
3 7 9 2 
3 7 9 2 
2 3 7 9 
```

### Reading the trace

- Step 1: `3` is smaller than `7`, so `7` shifts right and `3` goes first.
- Step 2: `9` is bigger than everything before it, so nothing moves.
- Step 3: `2` is smaller than all three, so all three shift and `2` lands at the front.

The part to the left of `i` is always sorted, and each step makes it one longer.

### Properties

- O(n²) in general, but O(n) on data that's already **almost sorted**, because each element only shifts a little.
- **Stable** (equal elements keep their order) and **in place** (no extra array).
- Fastest of all for tiny arrays. Real `std::sort` implementations switch to it for ranges below about 16 elements.

The number of shifts equals the number of pairs that are out of order (called **inversions**), which is why it measures how unsorted the input was.

**Your turn:** implement `insertion_sort` and return how many element **shifts** it performed (a measure of how unsorted the input was).

steps.seed:
#include <vector>

long insertion_sort(std::vector<int>& v) {

}

steps.solution:
#include <vector>

long insertion_sort(std::vector<int>& v) {
    long shifts = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 1; i < v.size(); i++) {
        int key = v[i];
        std::size_t j = i;
        while (j > 0 && v[j - 1] > key) {
            v[j] = v[j - 1];
            j--;
            shifts++;
        }
        v[j] = key;
    }
    return shifts;
}

steps.harness:
int main() {
    std::vector<int> a = {5, 2, 4, 6, 1, 3};
    long s = insertion_sort(a);
    CHECK_EQ("sorted", a, (std::vector<int>{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}));
    CHECK_EQ("shifts", s, 9L);
    std::vector<int> sorted = {1, 2, 3, 4};
    CHECK_EQ("already sorted: no shifts", insertion_sort(sorted), 0L);
    std::vector<int> rev = {4, 3, 2, 1};
    CHECK_EQ("reversed: every pair shifts", insertion_sort(rev), 6L);
    std::vector<int> empty;
    CHECK_EQ("empty", insertion_sort(empty), 0L);
}

steps.text:
**Merge sort** is the classic **divide and conquer** algorithm:

1. **Divide**: split the array into two halves.
2. **Conquer**: sort each half, recursively. An array of 0 or 1 elements is already sorted: that's the base case.
3. **Combine**: **merge** the two sorted halves into one sorted array.

The merge is the heart of it. With two sorted lists, the smallest remaining element is always at the front of one of them, so you repeatedly take the smaller front:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> merge(const std::vector<int>& a, const std::vector<int>& b) {
    std::vector<int> out;
    out.reserve(a.size() + b.size());
    std::size_t i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < a.size() && j < b.size()) {
        if (a[i] <= b[j]) out.push_back(a[i++]);    // <= keeps it stable
        else out.push_back(b[j++]);
    }
    while (i < a.size()) out.push_back(a[i++]);     // copy whatever is left
    while (j < b.size()) out.push_back(b[j++]);
    return out;
}

int main() {
    for (int x : merge({1, 4, 9, 10}, {2, 3, 9, 20, 21})) std::cout << x << " ";
    std::cout << "\n";
}
```

```text
1 2 3 4 9 9 10 20 21 
```

### Why O(n log n)

Halving repeatedly gives about log₂ n **levels** of recursion. At each level, the merges together touch every element once, which is O(n) work per level. So the total is O(n log n), and unlike quicksort that's true **even in the worst case**.

```
[5 2 8 1 9 3]
[5 2 8] [1 9 3]          split
[5] [2 8] [1] [9 3]      split
...                      single elements are sorted
[2 5 8] [1 3 9]          merge
[1 2 3 5 8 9]            merge
```

### Properties

- Always O(n log n).
- Stable, as long as the merge takes from the **left** half on ties (`<=`). That's why `std::stable_sort` is usually a merge sort.
- Needs O(n) extra memory for merging.

### Your task

Write a recursive helper that sorts `v[lo..hi)`: if the range has fewer than 2 elements, return; otherwise sort both halves and merge them through a temporary vector, copying the result back. Allocating one temporary buffer up front and reusing it is faster than making new vectors at every level.

**Your turn:** implement `merge_sort(std::vector<int>& v)`. The test sorts 200,000 numbers, so an O(n²) sort won't finish in time.

steps.seed:
#include <vector>

void merge_sort(std::vector<int>& v) {

}

steps.solution:
#include <vector>

static void sort_range(std::vector<int>& v, std::vector<int>& tmp, std::size_t lo, std::size_t hi) {
    if (hi - lo < 2) return;
    std::size_t mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    sort_range(v, tmp, lo, mid);
    sort_range(v, tmp, mid, hi);
    std::size_t i = lo, j = mid, k = lo;
    while (i < mid && j < hi) tmp[k++] = (v[j] < v[i]) ? v[j++] : v[i++];
    while (i < mid) tmp[k++] = v[i++];
    while (j < hi) tmp[k++] = v[j++];
    for (std::size_t x = lo; x < hi; x++) v[x] = tmp[x];
}

void merge_sort(std::vector<int>& v) {
    std::vector<int> tmp(v.size());
    sort_range(v, tmp, 0, v.size());
}

steps.harness:
#include <algorithm>
int main() {
    std::vector<int> a = {38, 27, 43, 3, 9, 82, 10};
    merge_sort(a);
    CHECK_EQ("small", a, (std::vector<int>{3, 9, 10, 27, 38, 43, 82}));
    std::vector<int> e;
    merge_sort(e);
    CHECK("empty", e.empty());
