steps.solution:
#include <functional>
#include <queue>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

struct Edge {
    int from, to;
    long long cost;
};

std::vector<long long> dijkstra(int n, const std::vector<Edge>& edges, int src) {
    std::vector<std::vector<std::pair<int, long long>>> adj(n);
    for (const auto& e : edges) adj[e.from].push_back({e.to, e.cost});
    const long long INF = -1;
    std::vector<long long> dist(n, INF);
    using Item = std::pair<long long, int>;
    std::priority_queue<Item, std::vector<Item>, std::greater<Item>> heap;
    dist[src] = 0;
    heap.push({0, src});
    while (!heap.empty()) {
        auto [d, v] = heap.top();
        heap.pop();
        if (d > dist[v]) continue;
        for (auto [w, cost] : adj[v]) {
            long long nd = d + cost;
            if (dist[w] == INF || nd < dist[w]) {
                dist[w] = nd;
                heap.push({nd, w});
            }
        }
    }
    return dist;
}

steps.harness:
int main() {
    std::vector<Edge> e = {{0, 1, 4}, {0, 2, 1}, {2, 1, 2}, {1, 3, 1}, {2, 3, 5}};
    auto d = dijkstra(5, e, 0);
    CHECK_EQ("to 1 via 2", d[1], 3LL);
    CHECK_EQ("to 3", d[3], 4LL);
    CHECK_EQ("source", d[0], 0LL);
    CHECK_EQ("unreachable", d[4], -1LL);
    int n = 100000;
    std::vector<Edge> chain;
    for (int i = 0; i + 1 < n; i++) {
        chain.push_back({i, i + 1, 1000000});
        if (i + 2 < n) chain.push_back({i, i + 2, 1999999});
    }
    auto far = dijkstra(n, chain, 0);
    CHECK_EQ("100,000 vertices", far[n - 1], 99999LL * 1000000 - 49999);
}

steps.text:
Some problems are about groups that keep **merging**: which computers can reach each other as cables are added, which accounts belong to the same person as links are found. Re-running a BFS after every merge would be slow. **Union-find** (also called disjoint set union) answers "are these two in the same group?" and "merge these two groups" in nearly O(1) each.

Each group is stored as a tree through a `parent` array. The **root** of a tree identifies its group: `find(x)` follows parents up to the root. Two items are in the same group exactly when they have the same root, and merging two groups just points one root at the other.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <numeric>
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> parent;

int find(int x) {
    while (parent[x] != x) x = parent[x];     // walk up to the root
    return x;
}

void unite(int a, int b) {
    parent[find(a)] = find(b);               // hang one root under the other
}

int main() {
    parent.resize(6);
    std::iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);   // everyone is their own group: 0 1 2 3 4 5
    unite(0, 1);
    unite(2, 3);
    unite(1, 3);                                  // joins {0,1} with {2,3}
    std::cout << (find(0) == find(2)) << (find(0) == find(4)) << "\n";

    int groups = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) groups += (find(i) == i);   // count roots
    std::cout << groups << " groups\n";
}
```

```text
10
3 groups
```

The groups are `{0, 1, 2, 3}`, `{4}` and `{5}`.

### Making it fast

This simple version can build long chains, making `find` O(n). Two tricks fix that:

- **Union by size**: keep a `size` array, and always attach the **smaller** tree under the root of the larger one. Trees then stay shallow (height at most log n).
- **Path compression**: during `find`, after locating the root, point every node you passed **directly** at it. The next `find` on any of them is a single step.

```cpp
int find(int x) {
    int root = x;
    while (parent[root] != root) root = parent[root];
    while (parent[x] != root) {            // second pass: repoint the path
        int next = parent[x];
        parent[x] = root;
        x = next;
    }
    return root;
}
```

Together they make each operation effectively constant time. Keep a `groups_` counter that starts at `n` and drops by one on every successful merge, and read a set's size from its root.

**Your turn:** write class `UnionFind`:

- `explicit UnionFind(int n)`: n separate sets
- `int find(int x)` with path compression
- `bool unite(int a, int b)`: merge; returns false if already in the same set
- `int groups() const`: number of separate sets
- `int size_of(int x)`: size of x's set

